<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Narapatisithu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1150–<a href="/years/1211">1211</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Sithu II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Narapatisithu, also known as Sithu II, was king of Pagan from 1174 to 1211, a reign often seen as the height of the kingdom. He restored stability after a period of violent successions, organised a palace guard, supported the reform of the monastic order by monks ordained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, and built the Sulamani temple. Burmese increasingly replaced Mon as the main written language under his rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/narapatisithu.jpg" alt="Narapatisithu" width="320" height="196" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jialiang Gao (peace-on-earth.org) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gawdawpalin_Temple_Bagan_Myanmar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Narapatisithu was born in Pagan on 8 October 1150 to Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narathu">Narathu</a> and his wife, later known as Myauk Pyinthe, Queen of the Northern Palace; the chronicles disagree on his dates of birth and reign. When his elder brother Naratheinkha became king in 1171 and faced revolts by the Kudus of the Tagaung region and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mons">Mons</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanintharyi-region">Tenasserim</a> coast, he made Narapatisithu heir apparent and commander-in-chief.</p><p>In 1174 Naratheinkha seized his brother's wife Weluwaddy while Narapatisithu was away on a mission. Narapatisithu sent a band of 80 men led by Aungzwa to kill the king, and then took the throne as Sithu II, in honour of his grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaungsithu">Alaungsithu</a>, with the regnal name Śrī Tribhuvanāditya Pavaradhammarāja.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After the unrest that had followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaungsithu">Alaungsithu</a>'s death in 1167, he persuaded a great-grandson of the Mon king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuha">Manuha</a> not to rebel, and the rest of the reign saw no revolts. He built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyaukse">Kyaukse</a> weir and began the Mu canals in the present-day Shwebo District, though repeated attempts to carry irrigation into Minbu District failed. Besides the Sulamani he built the Gawdawpalin, Minmalaung, Dhammayazika and Chaukpala temples at Pagan.</p><p>His chief justice was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/htilominlo">Nadaungmya</a> and his chief minister Ananda Thuriya. The first Burmese customary law, based on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaungsithu">Alaungsithu</a>'s judgments, was compiled and applied across the kingdom, and the term Mranma for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamar-people">Burmans</a> was used openly in Burmese inscriptions. Through the primate Shin Uttarajiva most monks aligned with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavihara">Mahavihara</a> school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>. The Pali Culavamsa tells of a Sinhalese raid on the coast in 1180; the Burmese chronicles do not mention it, and Htin Aung and Wilhelm Geiger judged the account conjectural or much exaggerated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Sithu II died on 18 August 1211. On his deathbed, it is told, he placed the hands of his five sons on his chest and urged them to rule with mercy and justice and to live in brotherly love. He is remembered as the last important king of Pagan, and he is venerated as a nat, a spirit of Burmese folk religion, at the Kuni Shrine in Pakhan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Narapatisithu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Narapatisithu</b>, ruling 1174–1211, reorganised the palace guard and supported a reform of the monastic order in which monks ordained in Sri Lanka, led by Chapata, introduced the Mahavihara lineage.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pagan-kingdom">Pagan Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bagan, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. Died in Bagan, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.17,94.86&amp;year=1189" aria-label="Bagan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7100 1381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Narapatisithu"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7550 1651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7550 1651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.17,94.86&amp;year=1189">What was here in 1189? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
